; ======================================================================
;  hw_board.pi4 - the Pi 4 backend for the HwBoard* seam.
;
;  Three facts the payload ABI (Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi) needs and no command
;  family does: what this board is called, how to reset it, and what state
;  its MMU and caches are in.
;
;  THEY HAVE NO #CAP_ FLAG, for the same reason HwAddr* has none: every
;  board has an identity, a way to reset and a cache state, so there is
;  nothing that could be absent. What differs is what the board KNOWS, and
;  that is an answer, not a capability.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4 (SafetyToFirmware),
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4 (MmuSctlrEl2) and RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4
;  (gCacheOn), and before Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi, which calls all three.
;  House rule 10: this file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwBoardName()
;
;  THE FULL MARKETING NAME AND NOT A SHORT CODE, because the one place it
;  is rendered is a diagnostic screen or a roadside inspection screen,
;  where a person reads it. A payload that wants to make a DECISION asks
;  SvcCapGet, never this.
;
;  Returned as a POINTER. Printed with UartWriteStr and never Print() -
;  Print picks its formatter from the argument TYPE, an untyped pointer is
;  not a .s, and Print(p) would put the ADDRESS on the screen in decimal.
;  The trap is documented at PutClockRow, at UsbDiskUp and in
;  settings_cmd.pi4; this is it avoided once more.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwBoardName()
  ProcedureReturn "Raspberry Pi 4 Model B"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwReboot() - reset the board. Does not return.
;
;  THE SAME PATH `reset` TAKES, and deliberately the same one: two ways to
;  reset a board are two behaviours to keep in step, and the day they
;  diverge is the day a payload's reset leaves something running that the
;  operator's reset stops.
;
;  SafetyToFirmware lights the PM watchdog's fuse and spins; the firmware
;  then reloads KERNEL8.IMG, which is this monitor. Nothing in memory
;  survives, which is the point.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwReboot()
  UartDrain()
  SafetyToFirmware()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMmuState() - bit 0 MMU on, bit 1 D-cache on, bit 2 I-cache on.
;
;  READ OUT OF THE LIVE SCTLR RATHER THAN OUT OF gCacheOn, and that
;  difference is the whole reason this is a seam and not a getter on a
;  global. gCacheOn is what the MONITOR BELIEVES it did; the register is
;  what the PROCESSOR IS ACTUALLY DOING. A payload asking this question
;  is asking the second one - it has just been handed a machine that a
;  `cache off`, a RunAt handover and possibly its own earlier code have
;  all touched, and the honest answer is the register.
;
;  WHICH SCTLR, and it is no longer a fixed answer. 2026-09-07: Anvil
;  runs at EL3 under our own arm stub and at EL2 under the stock one, so
;  this asks MmuSctlr() (RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4) for the SCTLR OF THE
;  LEVEL THE MONITOR IS AT. It said MmuSctlrEl2(), which at EL3 reads a
;  register the processor is not translating through and which the stub
;  leaves holding $30C50830 - so a payload would have been told the MMU
;  and both caches were off at the exact moment `cache on` had turned
;  them on. This procedure exists to refuse that kind of answer.
;
;  THE BIT POSITIONS DO NOT CHANGE WITH THE LEVEL. M is bit 0, C is bit
;  2 and I is bit 12 in SCTLR_EL2 and in SCTLR_EL3 alike - ARM ARM
;  D13.2.106 and D13.2.107, and the two registers share their RES1
;  pattern for the same reason. They are remapped onto the ABI's own
;  0/1/2 here so that the payload never learns an ARM field number,
;  which is exactly what the portable vocabulary is for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMmuState()
  Define s.i
  Define r.i
  s = MmuSctlr()
  r = 0
  If (s & 1) <> 0
    r = r | 1
  EndIf
  If (s & 4) <> 0
    r = r | 2
  EndIf
  If (s & 4096) <> 0
    r = r | 4
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMicros() - monotonic microseconds since Anvil started.
;
;  Anvil/Lib/timer.pi4's Micros() exactly, exported under the seam name so
;  that Anvil/Hal/abi.pbi - which is core and compiles for both boards -
;  does not have to name a Pi 4 library. NEVER GOES BACKWARDS AND NEVER
;  JUMPS: it is the architectural counter, zero-based per boot, and it is
;  the only clock the payload ABI promises.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMicros()
  ProcedureReturn Micros()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwPet() - pet the deadman.
;
;  ANVIL NEVER PETS IT ON THE PAYLOAD'S BEHALF - the payload calls this in
;  its own loop, and that is the whole reason the deadman makes a bricked
;  truck impossible. A monitor that petted a watchdog for a program it is
;  not running has disarmed the one thing standing between a hung payload
;  and a board that needs a laptop.
;
;  Safe when nothing is armed: SafetyWatchdogPet returns immediately.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwPet()
  SafetyWatchdogPet()
EndProcedure
